WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:08.070
Welcome.

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:09.410
My name is Kate Hertweck.

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.480
I'm the bioinformatics
training manager

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.160
at Fred Hutchinson
Cancer Research

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:15.590
Center in Seattle, Washington.

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:19.220
My career focus is helping
scientists learn the coding

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:21.920
and data skills
they need to apply

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.990
cutting-edge
computational methods

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:26.780
and accelerate the
rate of science.

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:28.550
I'm excited to
tell you about one

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:30.770
of the best parts of my job--

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:34.460
helping researchers unlock
the mindset and tools

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.770
that help them make
the most of their data

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:40.170
and get more work
done in less time.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:43.170
Here's how the general
process of teaching someone R

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:45.390
usually happens.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:47.730
I work for a biomedical
research center

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:50.490
with a huge breadth
of research topics.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.640
But everyone wants to make
figures for publication.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.060
My community loves R,
especially tidyverse,

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.770
because it makes
sense to them to be

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.050
able to achieve those goals.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.330
In my introductory
R class, I start

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:06.810
by teaching some general
skills in data manipulation

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.720
and filtering.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:12.500
My participants learn to
create elegant, effective data

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:16.580
visualizations that help
them explore their data.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:19.340
After a few hours bent
over their keyboards,

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.330
I can see the slowly dawning
realization in their eyes.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:25.670
With the power
unlocked by coding,

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:28.670
I will surely obtain a
statistically significant

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:31.700
result supporting my
insightful hypothesis,

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:34.430
yielding fame and glory,
but more importantly

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:39.710
a paper in Nature or Science as
well as future grant funding.

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:41.870
In case you're not a
scientist, and this

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:44.060
doesn't resonate
with you, let's think

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:49.730
about it from a layperson's,
well, lay dog's perspective.

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:52.550
For scientists, unlocking
the power of coding

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:55.790
is like when we're dogs getting
a one-way ticket to the dog

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:59.120
park complete with all of
our favorite toys and lots

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:02.510
of interesting things to sniff.

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:08.229
That moment when the dog
realizes the dog park is near

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:12.100
is like that moment when an
important concept about data

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:16.060
or computational work clicks
for someone learning R

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:20.880
and they see a world of
opportunity open up for them.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.630
Unfortunately, all too
often, when the time

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:28.470
comes to apply their
skills to their own data,

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:30.330
they're confronted
with a console

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:32.790
that looks more like this.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:35.760
These red error messages
are sometimes scary,

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:40.080
often confusing, and
always frustrating.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.030
The prevalent feeling
they experience

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:44.580
is disappointment
tinged with a touch

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:47.550
of how am I supposed to
achieve fame and glory when

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:51.920
I can't even get R to
acknowledge my data exists?

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.120
That crushing defeat in
the face of such promise

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.190
is also something a
dog can relate to.

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:01.590
It's the equivalent
of jumping in the car,

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:03.470
thinking we're heading
to the dog park.

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:06.680
We can almost feel the
breeze, taste the rubber ball

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:09.110
in our mouth, and
smell the grass,

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:12.120
instead ending up at the vet.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:16.590
This is exactly the
opposite of what we wanted.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:19.290
It's disappointing
and demoralizing

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.870
so much so that we don't
want to get in the car again,

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:29.050
even if we might end up at the
dog park eventually someday.

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:31.960
So how do we jump
over this barrier,

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:37.250
restoring a belief that we
can achieve those lofty goals?

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:39.020
When I accepted my
current position

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:41.840
to develop and implement
a training program

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:46.190
in reproducible computational
methods 2 and 1/2 years ago,

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:49.130
I thought the most
important part of my job

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:52.070
would be time spent
in a classroom.

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:53.960
I had spent the
previous four years

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:57.050
teaching computational skills
to students at the university

00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:57.920
level.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.980
And after an entire
career in academia,

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:02.990
I thought that
the highest impact

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:04.940
I could have at
a research center

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:09.960
would involve teaching formerly
structured short courses.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:12.480
During my career,
I've had the privilege

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.940
of personally teaching
hundreds of people

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:18.300
to code in R. Especially
in my last few years

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.839
focusing on adult learners
outside of formal university

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:24.210
courses, I've learned
a lot about what

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:27.600
it takes to help people
succeed in learning R.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.180
And that success is
not solely reliant

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.210
on formal short courses.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:36.690
Now, I define success as
whether people continue

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:39.960
to work with R after
leaving my classroom

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:43.380
or at least if they gain some
general literacy and data

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:46.620
and computing skills so they
can work more effectively

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.410
with computational staff.

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:52.800
Given the breadth of research
topics members of my community

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.610
pursue, I am faced with
the monumental task

00:04:56.610 --> 00:05:00.210
of helping people apply R skills
to many different types of data

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:02.550
and research questions.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:05.280
The question I then
continually ask myself

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:09.870
as I prepare to deliver
training materials is this.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:12.780
What prevents people
from using these tools

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:15.430
after they've learned them?

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:18.690
Let's take a quick tour of the
process of gaining expertise

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:22.900
and think about what this
looks like with coding.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:25.390
Regardless of the
prior education someone

00:05:25.390 --> 00:05:27.760
possesses in a
different field, I

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:30.730
consider a person who has
no prior experience learning

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:33.280
to code in R a novice.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:35.830
In my community, that
means they probably don't

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:39.430
have any experience coding
in a different language

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:41.140
and probably also
don't know much

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:45.040
about how to structure and
store their data effectively.

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:48.520
I integrate general concepts
in coding and data management

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:52.780
into my intro R course and focus
on directly applicable skills

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:55.480
like those I showed
in code earlier.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:58.330
By the time someone has
completed my introductory R

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:00.220
class, I like to
think that they're

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:04.270
no longer a novice since
they have now transitioned

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:06.460
to competent practitioner.

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:09.160
I define a competent
practitioner as someone

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:12.850
who applies R code to their
own research problems.

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:16.270
A practitioner probably doesn't
know if the way that they code

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:19.300
represents the most elegant
or efficient solution

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:20.590
to a problem.

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:23.120
But they get the job done.

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:25.670
I consider expert-level
coders to be

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:29.120
people who are writing code that
make it easy for other people

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:30.710
to use it.

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:35.210
They encapsulate their projects
in fully documented R packages

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:38.480
and try to apply best
practices in coding to make

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:42.690
it robust and reproducible.

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:46.470
To be clear, I'm less concerned
about formally categorizing

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:48.270
people into these groups.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:50.370
I think more about
how to help people

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:52.680
follow the arrows to the right.

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:55.140
In practice, most
people I encounter

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:58.530
are practitioners of
some type most often

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:02.040
interested in getting
the immediate job done.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:04.320
Regardless, this model
is a really useful way

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:06.700
to think about a
few key questions.

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:08.790
What do people need to learn?

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:12.860
And how are they most likely
to learn these things?

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:14.610
We're going to investigate
these questions

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:17.760
by visiting a few colleagues
of mine who generally represent

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:21.350
each of these categories.

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:23.330
Please meet Ash.

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:26.390
Ash is trained in
software engineering

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:29.360
and currently works in data
management and software

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:32.420
development in a research
lab writing software

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:35.540
for scientists to use
for research purposes.

00:07:35.540 --> 00:07:38.780
Ash is writing a
package named borkr.

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:41.690
Borkr creates maps
indicating density

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:44.150
of dogs being walked in
certain areas of a city

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:45.620
at certain times.

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:49.250
Ash hopes that borkr will be
used for both public dog health

00:07:49.250 --> 00:07:53.090
tracking as well as integration
with the dog social media

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:55.730
network Snootbork.

00:07:55.730 --> 00:07:58.550
However, Ash hasn't worked
much with maps lately.

00:07:58.550 --> 00:08:01.220
And Ash needs to know the
best approaches in borkr

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:03.440
to achieve their goals.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:06.890
What Ash does is go and asks
on Twitter and some local Slack

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:09.420
communities for their
recommendations.

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:13.040
They are able to narrow down
the options available based

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:16.220
on what they and their friends
understand about the project

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:18.180
requirements.

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:21.030
Ash goes ahead and references
the relevant documentation

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:22.650
for each option.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:24.300
And given their
breadth of knowledge,

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:27.330
Ash can quickly identify the
relevant functions and how

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:30.450
to connect them together
to implement mapping

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:34.080
in the context of borkr.

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:37.030
One of his colleagues
is named Avery.

00:08:37.030 --> 00:08:39.929
Avery's a graduate student
with some formal coursework

00:08:39.929 --> 00:08:42.809
in data science but is still
learning the best methods

00:08:42.809 --> 00:08:46.650
for optimizing
workflows in R. Avery

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:50.550
is developing documentation
for a group of summer interns

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:53.790
and wants to streamline the
process of creating projects

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:55.290
with them.

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:58.050
Avery goes and asks
Ash who recommends

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:00.220
the package use this.

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:02.550
Avery is really excited,
goes and takes a look

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:07.020
at the main documentation page,
and is really intimidated.

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:09.690
They haven't worked with a
package like this before.

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:13.290
And they also haven't worked
much with Git or GitHub, which

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:16.740
is one of the really awesome
features involved in use this.

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:19.680
They especially aren't
comfortable in their ability

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:24.120
to use this package and help
other people use the tool as

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:25.870
well.

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:29.130
So Avery comes to my office
hours and asks for help.

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:33.600
I point them towards a project
from our colleagues' lab

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:37.144
that actually applies use this.

00:09:37.144 --> 00:09:39.830
This particular
project is a template

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:45.710
that includes working with
GitHub, starting an analysis,

00:09:45.710 --> 00:09:49.220
and explaining why their
particular approach works

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:51.740
for data analysis projects.

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:54.500
The explanation Avery
needed didn't just

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:57.740
include the code to run
but an example of how

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:02.060
all the pieces would fit
together in a final project.

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:04.460
Avery's really excited
about this breakthrough

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:08.990
and uses the package to support
their summer intern Quinn.

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:11.030
Quinn is an
undergraduate in biology

00:10:11.030 --> 00:10:14.510
who has recently started
learning computational methods.

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:17.780
Quinn thought my intro R
class was quite intuitive

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:21.780
and is eager to apply their
skills to their summer project.

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:24.020
However, they ended
up encountering

00:10:24.020 --> 00:10:26.930
the same problem I showed at
the beginning of this talk--

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:31.430
errors associated with read_csv.

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:34.880
Now, Quinn knows how to
use the help function

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:38.650
to share information about it.

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:41.680
And then they encounter
what is, in my opinion,

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:44.160
one of the great
contradictions of computing.

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:47.610
The simpler a function
seems, the more complicated

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:50.190
the help documentation
appears, especially

00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:52.500
to someone new to coding.

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:55.410
Quinn's reaction is similar
to the general response

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:58.110
I get from a novice
learner's first glance

00:10:58.110 --> 00:11:00.060
at this help documentation--

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:04.660
a bit of abject horror
thinking what have I done.

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:07.150
However, Quinn goes
and talks to Avery,

00:11:07.150 --> 00:11:09.940
who helps them think about
the basics of R coding,

00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:13.660
the structure of their data, and
how their project is organized,

00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:16.000
ultimately helping
them understand

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:18.220
how to piece together
that information

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:21.280
to import their data.

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:23.530
These, of course,
are the problems

00:11:23.530 --> 00:11:27.220
with which we all grapple
on a nearly daily basis.

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:31.060
How do we prioritize how to
exert our energy, especially

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:34.370
when we have different goals
with competing interests

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:37.300
and it's not clear whether or
not the effort we're exerting

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:40.720
is going to make a huge
difference in the long run?

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:44.080
These questions include things
like, do I write a vignette

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:47.230
or add a new feature to
the package I'm developing?

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:48.940
Do I keep trying to
get this function

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:50.230
to work for my analysis?

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:53.020
Or do I give up and
try something else?

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:58.570
How do I even find the help I
need to address this key issue?

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:01.510
As it turns out, even
small differences

00:12:01.510 --> 00:12:03.970
in how support
materials are developed

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:06.940
can make the difference
between someone struggling

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:09.610
and wrestling with
implementing a new approach

00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:13.360
or being able to take
the ball and run with it.

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:16.030
This is because,
in my experience,

00:12:16.030 --> 00:12:21.470
learning specific
technical skills is easy.

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:25.370
However, once you've learned
how a technical skill generally

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:29.960
operates, being able to apply
that to your own problem

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:33.430
is much more difficult.
The usefulness

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:36.730
of training and documentation
depends on both what

00:12:36.730 --> 00:12:42.250
information is provided
and how it's delivered.

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:44.560
Now that we have an idea
of the problem we face,

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:46.970
how do we actually
meet these needs?

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:49.630
So far, I've made some
generally vague references

00:12:49.630 --> 00:12:52.763
to training materials
and documentation.

00:12:52.763 --> 00:12:55.180
I think it's worth highlighting
what some of these options

00:12:55.180 --> 00:12:56.110
include.

00:12:56.110 --> 00:12:57.850
And if you'd like
more information

00:12:57.850 --> 00:13:00.580
on detailed descriptions
of types of materials,

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:02.590
head over to the
resources I've included

00:13:02.590 --> 00:13:06.280
in RStudio's collection
of conference materials.

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:10.300
First, manuals-- we have
a baseline expectation

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:13.360
in the community that a
standardized user manual

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:17.510
documenting features of
a package is included.

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:20.630
This particular documentation
provides basic information

00:13:20.630 --> 00:13:22.460
like how does a
function work and what

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:25.460
options are available for
working with a function.

00:13:25.460 --> 00:13:30.230
In my experience, manuals are
most useful for expert users

00:13:30.230 --> 00:13:33.140
or people who have some
familiarity with how

00:13:33.140 --> 00:13:36.620
the particular type
of function can work.

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:39.700
Another type of
material is vignettes.

00:13:39.700 --> 00:13:42.970
Vignettes answer questions like,
how do functions work together

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:45.400
to answer general questions?

00:13:45.400 --> 00:13:48.310
These tend to be more
like demos or tutorials

00:13:48.310 --> 00:13:50.440
for implementing
code, which ends up

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:55.330
making them much more accessible
for a broad variety of users.

00:13:55.330 --> 00:14:00.280
A third type of support material
are example projects or code.

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:04.120
These demonstrate what an
applicable authentic project

00:14:04.120 --> 00:14:06.980
incorporating a tool looks like.

00:14:06.980 --> 00:14:09.250
And these end up being
very crucial, especially

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:11.170
for practitioners.

00:14:11.170 --> 00:14:15.520
Fourth, formerly structured
in-depth short courses

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:19.420
are generally in highest demand
for novices and often something

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:21.280
that most people
are familiar with.

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:25.780
They would like someone to
tell them what the answers are.

00:14:25.780 --> 00:14:27.430
Now, each of these
different approaches

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:30.340
requires a different
amount of effort

00:14:30.340 --> 00:14:33.160
to both find if you're
wanting to learn from it

00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:36.190
or develop if you
are creating it.

00:14:36.190 --> 00:14:39.430
And sometimes, these
resources do blend together.

00:14:39.430 --> 00:14:42.430
They're also generally
supplemented by conversations

00:14:42.430 --> 00:14:44.620
with colleagues,
which I've found

00:14:44.620 --> 00:14:49.580
is one of the most valuable
ways to fill in knowledge gaps.

00:14:49.580 --> 00:14:51.620
Now that we've considered
how types of support

00:14:51.620 --> 00:14:56.090
vary based on levels of
expertise, let's return to Ash.

00:14:56.090 --> 00:14:59.940
Ash is excited that their
package is nearing completion.

00:14:59.940 --> 00:15:03.380
So they share the manual
with Avery and Quinn.

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:07.010
Avery has trouble understanding
how some of the borkr functions

00:15:07.010 --> 00:15:09.500
work but learns a lot
in the process of trying

00:15:09.500 --> 00:15:11.300
to figure it out.

00:15:11.300 --> 00:15:16.580
Quinn doesn't understand any of
it, including that Ash actually

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:20.420
wrote the package,
and then Quinn

00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:23.400
tries to Google it to
find more information.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:26.400
They come across search results
for a Chinese death metal band

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:27.580
by the same name.

00:15:27.580 --> 00:15:30.150
And Ash ultimately regrets
not googling the name

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:32.700
before deciding on
it for their package.

00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:35.070
At this point,
Ash has a choice--

00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:39.030
continue the package development
to integrate with Snootbork

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:42.720
or make it clearer what the
package is doing right now.

00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:45.540
Ash decides to ask
Avery to collaborate

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:48.360
on explaining how
functions fit together more

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:50.430
clearly in a vignette.

00:15:50.430 --> 00:15:53.160
They're supported by another
of Ashley's colleagues

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:56.550
who have developed some
code using borkr, which

00:15:56.550 --> 00:16:00.240
Ash is happy to reference in the
documentation for the package.

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:02.400
Quinn keeps attending
meetings and learns a lot

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:04.380
about the process of
code collaboration

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:08.220
and eventually even clarifies a
few instructions in the manual.

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:10.800
This story from my
colleagues is one example

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:14.370
of the process of
developing documentation.

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:17.430
What does it mean in
broader practice though?

00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:20.910
There are countless online
classes, tutorials, demos,

00:16:20.910 --> 00:16:22.740
and other resources
that can help

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:26.160
you learn R. These are
approaches that walk us

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:29.040
through the process
of some coding tasks,

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:31.950
essentially leading
you by a leash.

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:34.590
Given that availability,
why do people

00:16:34.590 --> 00:16:37.040
keep coming into my classroom?

00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:39.080
What I've learned
really makes or breaks

00:16:39.080 --> 00:16:41.780
progression to higher
levels of expertise

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:45.530
is access to information that
helps fill in the biggest

00:16:45.530 --> 00:16:47.870
gaps in our knowledge.

00:16:47.870 --> 00:16:50.540
My job as a trainer
is to provide guidance

00:16:50.540 --> 00:16:54.530
that fills in those gaps, which
allows learners to keep moving

00:16:54.530 --> 00:16:57.920
in the same direction
but without a leash

00:16:57.920 --> 00:17:03.320
so that they have guidance,
but more importantly, autonomy.

00:17:03.320 --> 00:17:06.109
So while I'm developing
support materials,

00:17:06.109 --> 00:17:09.170
I consider identifying
what knowledge

00:17:09.170 --> 00:17:13.190
is assumed in the
materials I'm developing

00:17:13.190 --> 00:17:16.579
as well as collecting
curated examples of code

00:17:16.579 --> 00:17:20.250
that apply methods commonly
desired in my community.

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:23.359
In fact, one of the most
effective things we can do

00:17:23.359 --> 00:17:27.140
is share high-quality training
materials with the people

00:17:27.140 --> 00:17:28.475
for whom they are most suited.

00:17:31.050 --> 00:17:34.680
The ecosystem of tools
available in the R community

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:37.710
really reflects the interests
of the people comprising

00:17:37.710 --> 00:17:39.210
that community.

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:41.760
With so many tools to
maintain and develop,

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:45.540
is it worth developing and
maintaining support materials

00:17:45.540 --> 00:17:47.140
too?

00:17:47.140 --> 00:17:49.810
Well, we share our
code so other people

00:17:49.810 --> 00:17:52.810
can benefit from our
hard work and to increase

00:17:52.810 --> 00:17:55.180
the impact of our efforts.

00:17:55.180 --> 00:17:58.000
If sharing our code
results in a multiplier

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:00.100
effect for our
effort, then making

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:04.870
it easier for people to use the
code multiplies it yet again.

00:18:04.870 --> 00:18:07.000
At a time when
disparities in the world

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:10.780
feel more stark than ever,
access to information

00:18:10.780 --> 00:18:13.630
makes a huge difference
in promoting equity

00:18:13.630 --> 00:18:16.310
for developing these skills.

00:18:16.310 --> 00:18:20.000
These values are why we
support open science projects.

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:21.970
And that means
considering how even

00:18:21.970 --> 00:18:26.740
our small actions can continue
to uphold these values.

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:30.010
In many cases, we don't even
have to create these materials

00:18:30.010 --> 00:18:31.210
ourselves.

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:35.560
Knowing what resources exist and
helping raise their visibility

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:39.320
can have a huge impact too.

00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:41.330
Regardless of what
level of expertise

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:45.860
you think best describes you,
please consider the following.

00:18:45.860 --> 00:18:48.110
For those of us
beginning to code in R,

00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:51.410
it's likely we'll encounter
a piece of documentation

00:18:51.410 --> 00:18:53.520
we don't understand.

00:18:53.520 --> 00:18:56.700
That's OK, and
it's not our fault.

00:18:56.700 --> 00:18:58.710
That information
probably isn't written

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:02.800
to be accessible for people
with our type of expertise.

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:05.670
There's almost certainly a
better resource out there

00:19:05.670 --> 00:19:09.680
or materials that can
help us bridge that gap.

00:19:09.680 --> 00:19:12.350
For those of us who are
practitioners worrying

00:19:12.350 --> 00:19:17.090
that even if our code works
it just isn't good enough,

00:19:17.090 --> 00:19:18.290
it's OK.

00:19:18.290 --> 00:19:21.110
Every expert coder has
been where we are now.

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:23.420
There's also no
requirement that we keep

00:19:23.420 --> 00:19:25.190
learning more advanced skills.

00:19:25.190 --> 00:19:27.590
But the community
is here to help us.

00:19:27.590 --> 00:19:31.300
And it'll probably be
very satisfying to learn.

00:19:31.300 --> 00:19:34.420
Finally, those of us who
are experts developing R

00:19:34.420 --> 00:19:37.270
packages unsure how to
make the information

00:19:37.270 --> 00:19:39.550
accessible to a broad audience--

00:19:39.550 --> 00:19:40.930
it's OK.

00:19:40.930 --> 00:19:42.580
This is a great
opportunity for us

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:46.660
to collaborate with someone with
a different type of expertise.

00:19:46.660 --> 00:19:50.140
These relationships can
help us share our tools

00:19:50.140 --> 00:19:53.590
more broadly while supporting
other community members

00:19:53.590 --> 00:19:57.090
and advancing their own skills.

00:19:57.090 --> 00:20:00.090
For all of us struggling
to communicate with people

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:03.720
who possess different
knowledge and skills, remember,

00:20:03.720 --> 00:20:06.450
this is something
everyone confronts.

00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:09.580
Given that we're all
continually learning things

00:20:09.580 --> 00:20:12.780
and, yes, also forgetting
things periodically,

00:20:12.780 --> 00:20:15.420
our own knowledge and
that of those around us

00:20:15.420 --> 00:20:19.320
is constantly changing too,
making for a sometimes rapidly

00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:20.790
moving target.

00:20:20.790 --> 00:20:23.250
One of the best things
we can do for each other

00:20:23.250 --> 00:20:25.980
is understand that we
can't expect a resource

00:20:25.980 --> 00:20:30.270
to be one size fits all and
that helping someone else learn

00:20:30.270 --> 00:20:33.150
is a way of supporting
the entire community

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:36.120
as well as yourself
in the future.

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:37.950
I learned about
the basic framework

00:20:37.950 --> 00:20:40.680
of levels of expertise
from The Carpentries.

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:43.860
This nonprofit group teaches
reproducible data and computing

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:48.060
skills, including R, to
communities worldwide and have

00:20:48.060 --> 00:20:51.690
a number of resources related
to training diverse audiences

00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:54.150
in technical skills.

00:20:54.150 --> 00:20:57.120
My current work at Fred Hutch
builds on methods and materials

00:20:57.120 --> 00:20:58.680
from The Carpentries.

00:20:58.680 --> 00:21:00.780
I've adapted their
workshop content

00:21:00.780 --> 00:21:04.620
to directly apply to the
research community I serve.

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:08.130
We otherwise apply the concepts
I've discussed here to training

00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:11.020
and community development.

00:21:11.020 --> 00:21:14.200
I'm also proud to be
advising MetaDocencia,

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:16.930
group focused on a different
type of targeted community

00:21:16.930 --> 00:21:20.080
development, supporting
educational practices

00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:22.090
for Spanish-speaking
communities.

00:21:22.090 --> 00:21:25.890
And this includes
teaching technical skills.

00:21:25.890 --> 00:21:29.580
Each of these groups facilitate
training for diverse audiences

00:21:29.580 --> 00:21:32.550
and demonstrate how
even small changes

00:21:32.550 --> 00:21:37.790
can mean a lot for individual
learners in the R community.

00:21:37.790 --> 00:21:40.370
If you'd like more information
about the ideas I've mentioned

00:21:40.370 --> 00:21:42.200
here, check out
the resources I've

00:21:42.200 --> 00:21:45.590
included in RStudio's collection
of conference materials.

00:21:45.590 --> 00:21:47.810
If you'd like to talk about
computational training

00:21:47.810 --> 00:21:51.290
and customizing our resources,
follow me on Twitter.

00:21:51.290 --> 00:21:53.480
I can also recommend
my Twitter account

00:21:53.480 --> 00:21:57.500
if you'd like to see pictures
of my favorite coworker Loki.

00:21:57.500 --> 00:22:01.790
He wouldn't consider himself
to be even a novice R coder.

00:22:01.790 --> 00:22:04.460
But he does listen very
intently to all of my thoughts

00:22:04.460 --> 00:22:07.610
about training and
community building.

00:22:07.610 --> 00:22:09.710
Thanks for watching
and for helping

00:22:09.710 --> 00:22:14.770
support a bigger, more
inclusive global R community.

